WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.280
If you learn one DAX function, make it CALCULATE.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.693
In this lesson, you will learn to build a total with SUM, filter that

3
00:00:09.693 --> 00:00:15.107
total with CALCULATE and FILTER, and label your cards so readers understand every number.

4
00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:17.040
Let's make your numbers smarter!

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.527
The card shows the overall shipment amount:

6
00:00:21.527 --> 00:00:23.865
about forty four point seven million.

7
00:00:23.865 --> 00:00:28.540
It comes from a simple measure, the SUM of the Amount column.

8
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.350
But what if we want the amount for just one salesperson, S P zero one?

9
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.317
I create a measure called SP01 Total Amount, using CALCULATE.

10
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:51.093
The first argument is what we want:

11
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:52.680
the SUM of Amount.

12
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.957
The second argument is the filter.

13
00:01:15.957 --> 00:01:19.367
FILTER takes the Shipments table, and the condition:

14
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:22.350
salesperson ID equals S P zero one.

15
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.760
CALCULATE then sums only the rows that pass.

16
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.108
I add the measure to a card, with a text box title, so readers

17
00:01:53.108 --> 00:01:54.789
know exactly what it means.

18
00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:57.142
The total for S P zero one:

19
00:01:57.142 --> 00:01:59.160
about three point six six million!

20
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.103
In Table view, here are the columns we used:

21
00:02:42.103 --> 00:02:47.040
shipment ID, salesperson ID, product, ship date, amount, boxes, and order status.

22
00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.535
That is the power of CALCULATE:

23
00:02:58.535 --> 00:03:03.160
the same SUM, a different filter, and a brand new answer. Well done!

